WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.560
A parent can gain a scar.

2
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.880
Why is a baby not born with that scar?

3
00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:07.700
Follow the gold mark on this skin.

4
00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:11.720
Then follow the blue gene cards toward a young animal.

5
00:00:15.200 --> 00:00:19.020
An inherited trait passes from parent to offspring through genes.

6
00:00:20.300 --> 00:00:22.440
An acquired trait develops during life.

7
00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:24.960
Eye color is inherited.

8
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:30.640
A scar, dyed hair, a learned skill, and exercise-built muscle are acquired.

9
00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:36.560
These labels describe where the characteristic comes from, not whether genes act

10
00:00:36.560 --> 00:00:37.560
alone.

11
00:00:38.033 --> 00:00:42.733
In people, mice, and many other animals, offspring begin from sex cells.

12
00:00:44.073 --> 00:00:45.793
Those cells carry the parents' genes.

13
00:00:47.293 --> 00:00:48.813
Skin cells can heal into a scar.

14
00:00:50.413 --> 00:00:52.193
Muscle cells can grow with exercise.

15
00:00:53.533 --> 00:00:57.653
Those body-cell changes do not change the genes inside the sex cells.

16
00:00:58.717 --> 00:01:02.797
The skin change and the gene cards now sit beside different paths.

17
00:01:04.117 --> 00:01:05.497
Which path can reach the offspring?

18
00:01:06.897 --> 00:01:08.957
Keep watching both routes before you decide.

19
00:01:14.950 --> 00:01:19.030
The blue cards pass through the sex-cell gate and continue into the young.

20
00:01:20.310 --> 00:01:23.110
The scar reaches a red stop at the body-cell gate.

21
00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.670
Gene-coded traits can pass on; ordinary changes gained during life cannot.

22
00:01:30.050 --> 00:01:32.970
Ordinary means body changes like the ones shown here.

23
00:01:33.933 --> 00:01:35.753
Genes are not the whole story.

24
00:01:37.073 --> 00:01:40.993
The environment is everything around a living thing that can affect it.

25
00:01:42.253 --> 00:01:45.513
Soil, light, water, food, and temperature belong to it.

26
00:01:46.793 --> 00:01:51.053
Genes and environment can work together to shape the phenotype, the trait that

27
00:01:51.053 --> 00:01:52.053
appears.

28
00:01:52.567 --> 00:01:56.547
Variation means differences among living things of the same kind.

29
00:01:57.907 --> 00:02:00.387
Some differences trace to inherited alleles.

30
00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:03.687
Others develop as environments differ.

31
00:02:04.947 --> 00:02:06.367
Many traits reflect both.

32
00:02:07.907 --> 00:02:12.187
Matching gene sets can meet different surroundings and produce different visible

33
00:02:12.187 --> 00:02:18.587
results. That is why tracing a cause works better than forcing every phenotype into

34
00:02:18.587 --> 00:02:21.807
a genes-only box or an environment-only box.

35
00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:25.920
Watch matching cuttings from a bigleaf hydrangea.

36
00:02:27.060 --> 00:02:28.820
Their blue gene cards stay the same.

37
00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:33.520
In many kinds, acidic soil lets roots take up more aluminum.

38
00:02:34.820 --> 00:02:36.480
That helps flowers become blue.

39
00:02:37.840 --> 00:02:39.900
Less acidic soil can give pink flowers.

40
00:02:41.180 --> 00:02:44.320
The soil changes the phenotype, not the cutting's genes.

41
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:49.830
Genes set which colors are possible, while soil helps decide which appears.

42
00:02:51.170 --> 00:02:55.890
The same logic explains why matching genes need not make matching phenotypes.

43
00:02:57.350 --> 00:03:00.130
Phenotype tells what appears, not which cause acted.

44
00:03:01.450 --> 00:03:02.490
First trace the cause.

45
00:03:03.770 --> 00:03:06.510
Then ask whether it can enter the gene route to offspring.
